# Commit on Every Change

After completing any code change in this project, create a git commit before finishing the task.

## When to Commit

- Commit after each logical unit of work (feature, fix, refactor, test, or docs update).
- If you make multiple related edits in one turn, commit once when the work is complete.
- Do not leave uncommitted changes at the end of a task unless the user explicitly asks you not to commit.

## Git Safety Protocol

- NEVER update the git config
- NEVER run destructive/irreversible git commands (push --force, hard reset, etc.) unless the user explicitly requests them
- NEVER skip hooks (--no-verify, --no-gpg-sign, etc.) unless the user explicitly requests it
- NEVER force push to main/master; warn the user if they request it
- Avoid git commit --amend unless all amend conditions in project/user rules are met
- Do not commit files that likely contain secrets (.env, credentials.json, etc.)
- Do not push to remote unless the user explicitly asks

## Commit Workflow

Run these in parallel before committing:

1. `git status` — see untracked and modified files
2. `git diff` — review staged and unstaged changes
3. `git log` — match recent commit message style

Then sequentially:

1. Stage relevant files (exclude secrets)
2. Commit with a concise 1–2 sentence message focused on **why**, not just what
3. Run `git status` to verify success

Use a HEREDOC for the commit message:

```bash
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
Brief summary of why this change was made.

EOF
)"
```

## If Commit Fails

- If a pre-commit hook rejects the commit, fix the issue and create a **new** commit (do not amend a failed commit)
- If there are no changes to commit, skip committing
